  password storage, no reset flow — and email addresses are exactly what sub-project D needs later anyway.

  What it costs you to go light now: upgrading later means backfilling identity onto existing change-log entries and
  task rows. That's annoying but bounded, as long as A stores a stable user id rather than a display name on every
  record. I'd design for that regardless.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should the app know who someone is? → Magic link (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m One more question before I put approaches on the table, then I'll stop asking and start proposing.

  It's about freshness, and it matters now rather than in sub-project C because it largely picks the backend. When a
  teammate changes a task, does your screen need to update on its own within a second or two, or is it acceptable that
  you see it on your next page load or refresh? Realtime feels alive and makes notifications land the moment they
  happen, but it means a persistent connection, reconnect handling, and reasoning about what happens to a screen that
  mutates under someone mid-edit. Load-time freshness is dramatically simpler and, for a handful of people working on a
  shared task list, often indistinguishable in practice — you're rarely both staring at the same task in the same ten
  seconds.

  The honest middle is polling every 20–30 seconds, which gets you most of the felt liveness for almost none of the
  complexity, and is trivial to swap for a real subscription later if it isn't enough.
